    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Do not accept this initial offer as-is, but do not walk away either. You should negotiate.** \n\nBecause Google reached out to you and you are happy at Meta, you have maximum leverage. The offer is a solid **at-market baseline** (~50th\u201360th percentile for an L6 Product Manager in the Bay Area), but standard first offers from Google leave substantial room for negotiation\u2014especially on **equity** and **sign-on bonus**.\n\n---\n\n### Compensation Comparison & The \"Hidden\" Vesting Math\n\nGoogle\u2019s offer looks like a modest step up on paper, but the actual cash flow differs significantly due to Google\u2019s front-loaded equity vesting schedule (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** over 4 years).\n\n| Component | Meta (Current IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Google Year 1 Vesting Math |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 |\n| **RSUs (Annualized / Y1)** | $250,000 / $200,000 | $270,000 (4-yr avg) | **$356,400** (33% of $1.08M) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | $0 |\n| **Total Year 1 Comp** | **~$476,000** | **~$552,000 (avg)** | **~$638,400 (actual Y1)** |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n1. **Year 1 & 2 boost:** Thanks to the 33/33/22/12 vesting curve, your actual take-home in Years 1 and 2 is roughly **~$638k/yr**.\n2. **Year 3 & 4 cliff:** In Year 4, the initial grant only vests 12% ($129.6k). The recruiter mentioned annual refreshes to offset this, but refreshes depend entirely on performance ratings and stack slowly over 4 years (refreshes vest 25% per year).\n3. **Base salary regression:** Your base pay would drop slightly from $240,000 to $235,000, despite the top of the posted range being $271,000.\n4. **Missing sign-on:** Google frequently omits sign-on bonuses in initial offers but uses them as a primary lever during negotiation.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nTarget the **75th+ percentile** for an L6 PM in the Bay Area:\n\n1. **Equity (GSUs): Ask for $1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** over 4 years (up from $1.08M).\n   * *Rationale:* This brings your Year 1/2 equity to ~$415k\u2013$430k and increases your 4-year floor.\n2. **Sign-on Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000**.\n   * *Rationale:* This offsets any unvested Meta RSUs you forfeit, bridges timing on your upcoming Meta bonus, and compensates for the transition risk.\n3. **Base Salary: Ask for $255,000 \u2013 $260,000**.\n   * *Rationale:* Your current base is $240,000, and Google\u2019s band goes up to $271,000. You should not take a base pay cut.\n\n---\n\n### Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nBecause you are not actively looking to leave Meta, maintain a collaborative, confident, and \"reluctant mover\" posture.\n\n#### Step 1: Schedule a Call with the Recruiter\nDo not negotiate via email if possible; schedule a 10-minute sync.\n\n#### Step 2: Anchor Your Value & Make the Ask\n> *\"Thank you again for the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the product scope at Google.*\n> \n> *As you know, I wasn't actively looking to leave Meta and I'm very well-positioned here. When evaluating the total package against my current trajectory, base compensation, and upcoming vesting schedule, the current offer doesn't quite provide the premium needed to make the move.*\n> \n> *If we can get the base salary to **$255,000**, increase the initial equity grant to **$1,250,000**, and include a **$75,000 sign-on bonus** to offset leaving Meta mid-cycle, I would be thrilled to sign immediately.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Qualitative Factors to Weigh\n\n* **Performance & Refreshers:** Meta\u2019s performance review cycles (PSC) generally offer higher multiplier upside on bonuses and equity refreshes for high performers compared to Google\u2019s GRAD system.\n* **Pace & Culture:** Meta PM culture is highly execution-focused, top-down, and fast-paced. Google tends to be more consensus-driven and engineering-led, which some PMs love and others find slower to navigate.\n* **Downside Risk:** In Big Tech hiring, **a polite counter-offer will not cause them to rescind the offer.** The worst they will say is, *\"This is the best we can do,\"* leaving you free to decide whether to take the current offer or stay at Meta.",
